    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Could you expand a bit more on that third point regarding the financial mechanisms? I'd like to explain to my students how things like credit or standardized exchange actually functioned in a world without modern banking. What were some of the more specific ways these early financial systems helped keep trade moving across such long distances?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the absence of modern banks, merchants often relied on bills of exchange, which were essentially written promises guaranteeing payment at a future date or in a different location. This allowed traders to move wealth without physically transporting large quantities of volatile specie, significantly reducing the risk of theft. Additionally, certain commercial hubs developed systems of deposit-taking and lending, functioning as proto-banks that facilitated credit necessary for long-term, large-scale caravans.",
      "source": "target",
