      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Merchant groups like the Sogdians were pivotal in establishing the trust networks necessary for these complex transactions across vast distances. They often acted as cosmopolitan intermediaries, possessing linguistic skills and established residential communities in diverse regions, which built essential bonds of mutual reliance. These ethnically and commercially bonded networks minimized risk through shared legal customs and established reputations, which functioned as an early form of transnational, non-state assurance.",
      "source": "target",
